WebKit: the classic drawing area paints the rows above and below the view ahead of a scroll ("overscan bands").

The LazyOS window shows the picture ahead of the web process while it scrolls (it answers the wheel at
once, the page tens or hundreds of milliseconds later), and could only show a checkerboard where that
uncovered rows the view had never had. With LAZYOS_WEBKIT_OVERSCAN=<view heights> in its environment the
web process now paints the document's rows just above and below the view whenever nothing else is to be
painted, a chunk at a time and nearest the view first (in the direction of the last scroll), and sends
each as an update of its own (UpdateInfo::documentRect, document coordinates) that the backing store does
not take; WKPageSetBandCallback hands them to the client.

The main frame's ScrollView reports repaints that far past its visible rect (it used to drop them at the
edge of the view), so the drawing area knows which bands went stale; stale bands are repainted at most
twice a second, so an animation below the view does not cost a repaint of it on every frame. A new size, a
horizontal scroll or a whole-view repaint makes them all stale. The view always comes first: a band is only
painted with no view update waiting, and one at a time (it takes the same DidUpdate turn as an update).

